Encapsulation

Encapsulating __age means that it cannot be accessed or altered externally. Information can then be safely retrieved using get_age().

Inheritance

Using ‘class Student(Person):’ inherits Person’s __init__, name and get_age() methods – no need to manually recreate these.

Polymorphism

Each subclass has its own role() method. Therefore, when person.role() is executed, the relevant role() for that object type will be used.

Abstraction

By inheriting from ABC and adding @abstractmethod, Person() cannot be directly instantiated. All subclasses are then required to have a role() method.

Combining features:

Whilst abstraction creates the structure and guidelines, encapsulation manages the common variable (__age). Inheritance allows this structure to be applied to different roles. Polymorphism then enables a single list to execute role() consistently for all roles.
